WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:01.120
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.490
I'm here at KubeCon Seattle.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.510
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.710
I'm hosting today's activities.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.780
I have Brian Grant over here.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.620
Brian has been with
the Kubernetes project

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.620
from the very beginning.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.830
He's got a very long list
of titles that I have here.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.902
He is the co-tech lead of Google
Kubernetes Engine, the co-chair

00:00:17.902 --> 00:00:19.860
of the Kubernetes Native
Special Interest Group

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.870
for SIG Architecture,
Kubernetes API approver.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.100
He is also on the Kubernetes
Steering Committee.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.046
And he is part of the CNCF
Technical Oversight Committee.

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:34.420
Brian, you've been with the
project for a very long time.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.910
Can you talk about
where it all started

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.340
and where we've come since?

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.407
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah, I've
been at Google 11 years.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:42.240
At this point, I feel
like I've been working

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.920
on this project for 10 years.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.300
I worked on Google's internal
container platform, Borg,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.830
before this, and founded the
Omega projects internally.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.620
And we talk about Kubernetes
being an open-source Borg.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.050
It's actually closer to
being an open-source Omega,

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.660
because a lot of the ideas
were inspired by ideas that we

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.610
developed further in Omega.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.830
And some of them, we
integrated back into Borg.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.880
And others were hard
to integrate into Borg.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.090
Around 2013 when Docker
really started to take off,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:19.400
we had started to work on
open-sourcing our own container

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.570
runtime.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.670
Let me contain that for you.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.930
And when we saw Docker
really had a much better user

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.640
experience, we were
a lot more focused

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.297
on resource utilization.

00:01:31.297 --> 00:01:33.380
We're going to talk about
this more in our keynote

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.030
this afternoon with Tim
Hockin and Clayton Coleman,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.830
so I won't give too
much away there.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.430
But it was immediately
obvious to us

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.910
that this would be really
useful to the world broadly.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.150
And there had been some
other open-source projects

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.010
in this area, like Mesos.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:55.010
But we had some different
ideas about what

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.870
we wanted to do that
were, again, inspired

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:01.640
by our learnings from
Borg and our work on Omega

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.460
that we thought would be
really valuable to people.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:10.310
So we started work on
the API design late 2013.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.620
Eventually, the open-source
project took off.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.920
We open-sourced it.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.730
The initial project
just had four APIs--

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.370
pod, replication
controller, service, and I

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.960
want to say minion.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.240
The endpoint API didn't
even exist yet, I think.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.280
But we knew there would
be more and more APIs.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.060
Borg also started with a
handful of resource types,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.710
but it wasn't as extensible.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:41.010
So we really ran into obstacles
trying to add new concepts,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:41.840
for example.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.780
So we wanted Kubernetes to have
at least an extensible concept

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.450
space where you could
create new resource

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.090
types for new concepts.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.520
So it wasn't actually super
extensible in the beginning.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.410
You had to extend
it with Go code.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.550
And that's where we
partnered with the Red Hat

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.330
folks, who were building
OpenShift based on Kubernetes.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.340
And we decided, at the
time, we would just

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.760
use in-process Go interfaces
as extensions for admission

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.980
control, for example,
for authentication

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:15.600
and authorization and for
resource implementations.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.360
But over time, we've
converted those

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.000
to out-of-process
extension mechanisms,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.300
like admission webhooks,
custom resource definitions,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.460
API aggregation, and so on.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:25.460
SAAD ALI: Do you think--

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.330
BRIAN GRANT: And that
was really motivated

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.760
by a lot of that internal
work at the beginning where

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.824
we were really lacking
those mechanisms.

00:03:30.824 --> 00:03:32.240
SAAD ALI: Do you
think that's what

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.130
led to the project's success?

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.410
If not, what else do you
think was a part of--

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.600
BRIAN GRANT: I think that
was a big part of it.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:45.380
We knew that just the ability
to launch and scale containers

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.350
was table stakes.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.570
That was the bare minimum.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.680
But people would need
a lot more than that.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.880
And indeed, it's expanded
into a portable cloud platform

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.294
with more than 60
APIs, at this point.

00:03:56.294 --> 00:03:57.710
So you can look
at cloud providers

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.450
and see all the different
services that they provide.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.020
And people want a
portable version of that

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.270
that they can run in
a consistent fashion

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.620
in any environment.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.410
And Kubernetes
provides that to them.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.140
So as people try to adapt
Kubernetes to more and more use

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.060
cases, they add more features.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.220
They also make it
more pluggable.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.730
As you know-- you've been
working on the storage

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:20.279
systems--

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.880
how many different storage
layers there are, right?

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.850
So the more different
kinds of environments

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.709
and underlying infrastructure
that Kubernetes can run on,

00:04:29.709 --> 00:04:32.000
the more broadly useful it
is, the bigger the community

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.680
and the bigger the ecosystem.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.610
And that has a synergistic,
virtuous cycle effect

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.420
where more people are
invested in adding value

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.840
to the ecosystem as a whole.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:43.640
SAAD ALI: So it
sounds like being

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:45.848
more extensible from the
beginning helped the project

00:04:45.848 --> 00:04:47.030
become more successful.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.500
Are there any
challenges associated

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.160
with trying to make the
project more and more modular?

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:51.910
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.980
So we have extension points in
a lot of places, at this point.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.150
We have CSI, CNI, CRI
at the node level.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.960
We also have the admission
hooks, admission authorization

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.730
hooks, authentication hooks.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.006
We have custom resource
definitions, API aggregation.

00:05:08.006 --> 00:05:10.130
So there are so many
different ways you can extend,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.152
and people swap out
whole components as well.

00:05:12.152 --> 00:05:13.610
People have swapped
out kube-proxy;

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.620
the scheduler; ingress,
obviously; autoscalers.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.190
So making Kubernetes
work similarly enough

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.230
that people can get that
consistent environment

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.617
that they value is a challenge.

00:05:26.617 --> 00:05:28.700
So this is why we started
the conformance program,

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.820
to help ensure API
compatibility and workload

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.340
portability across all
the different Kubernetes

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.000
distributions.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.780
I think we have about 70,
now, Kubernetes distributions

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.370
and services that have
demonstrated that they

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.620
can pass the conformance test.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.680
And we're actively working on
improving the conformance test

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.400
coverage for the things that
everybody uses and depends on,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.410
especially for the
things that are highly

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:54.140
pluggable in the
system, because that's

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.284
where behaviors are likely to
deviate from one or another.

00:05:57.284 --> 00:05:58.700
SAAD ALI: And for
those who aren't

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.491
familiar with the
conformance program, what

00:06:00.491 --> 00:06:01.744
is the end benefit to users?

00:06:01.744 --> 00:06:03.410
BRIAN GRANT: So the
end benefit to users

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.000
is that Kubernetes works
the way you expect.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.680
So if you pick up an
off-the-shelf configuration

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.370
for a Kubernetes
application or a Helm Chart,

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.000
it will work no matter which
Kubernetes flavor you're using.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.710
If you find a tool in the
ecosystem for monitoring

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.100
your application or load
balancing or debugging

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.970
or CI/CD or whatnot,
it will work

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.570
regardless of which Kubernetes
distribution or service--

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:36.680
or multiple services,
increasingly-- you are using.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:38.450
Because hybrid cloud is reality.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.590
Multi-cloud is a reality.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.130
And it's really Kubernetes
that's enabling that.

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:45.380
Containers give you some
amount of portability.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:46.460
They let you take--

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.960
they separate
operational concerns

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.580
from developer concerns.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.980
So you can carry the
same immutable container

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.080
image from development
to production

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.080
through binary
promotion process.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.010
And they also run on different
Linux operating systems

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.420
and in different
cloud environments.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.472
But to make your whole
application deployment work,

00:07:07.472 --> 00:07:08.930
you need a lot more
than just that.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.580
You need networking and
load balancing and storage,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.830
for example.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.640
So Kubernetes provides those
additional abstractions

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.784
that you need to
get true portability

00:07:19.784 --> 00:07:21.200
across the different
environments.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.116
Thanks, Dan.

00:07:22.116 --> 00:07:22.616
Yeah.

00:07:24.846 --> 00:07:27.470
SAAD ALI: So it sounds like the
conformance program is actually

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.860
going to help Kubernetes users
have a consistent, dependable

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.420
experience.

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.360
So I've been hearing more
and more beyond Kubernetes

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.610
about new projects, things
like Istio, Knative.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.220
Can you talk about those and
how they relate to Kubernetes

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.540
and how they fit into the
open-source ecosystem?

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:43.790
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:47.300
And Google started
these projects

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.570
because Kubernetes
has grown a lot,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.160
and it covers a lot
of surface area.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.200
But we actually do
have a defined scope.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.080
If you look on kubernetes.io,
we have a "What is Kubernetes"

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.880
doc that I wrote
a few years ago.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.810
It largely is still
accurate and up to date.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:08.130
I simplified it after last
year's keynote a little bit.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.000
But really, we have the
same vision for Kubernetes

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.640
as we always did,
which is it's a base

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.000
platform for managing all kinds
of containerized workloads.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.812
So if you need to have this
additional functionality--

00:08:22.812 --> 00:08:24.270
like CI/CD in
particular, there are

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.360
lots of different opinions
about how to do that.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.772
I am personally a fan of GitOps
and declarative configuration.

00:08:32.772 --> 00:08:34.480
But there are lots of
different workflows

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:40.320
that have to integrate with a
lot of different systems for--

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.340
different-sized
organizations, for example,

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:43.740
may have different workloads.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.040
Different types of applications
written in different languages

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.350
or different
application frameworks

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:53.970
may benefit from really
highly-tailored developer

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:54.867
workflows.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:56.700
And actually, it's
getting easier and easier

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.320
to build custom developer
workflows on Kubernetes because

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.420
of how much it provides
for you out of the box.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.810
Like, task distribution and
execution is a solved problem.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.620
You don't have to
worry about that.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.590
And that's what a
lot of platforms

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.350
focused on in the past, and
even a lot of CI/CD systems

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.000
and other types
of orchestrators.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.050
But Kubernetes just
does that for you

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:21.510
so you can focus on the
last mile of getting really

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:25.050
a highly-tailored workflow.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:31.120
So Knative raises
that level higher

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.930
by providing a workflow for
these stateless applications,

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:38.050
including things like functions
and arbitrary languages

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.920
because it runs containers.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.240
So if you want to use JavaScript
like every functions platform

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:45.020
supports, that's great.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.470
But if you actually
have a Ruby application,

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.387
you can run that too.

00:09:49.387 --> 00:09:51.220
SAAD ALI: And the idea
would be that Knative

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.200
deploys on top of
Kubernetes and gives you

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:55.170
some sort of
serverless framework?

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.090
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah, Knative
deploys on top of Kubernetes

00:09:58.090 --> 00:09:59.710
and provides some
of the behaviors

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:02.890
that people really expect
in a serverless platform--

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.810
like 0 to 1 activation,
for example.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.300
Because it actually
brings in the Istio piece,

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.810
which is the service
mesh, which gives you

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:15.310
a lot more observerability and
control over the communication

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:16.665
between the services.

00:10:16.665 --> 00:10:20.950
So you know that
a request is being

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.660
delivered to an application.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.940
You can then spin up that
application on demand,

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.790
as just one example.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.350
And you can
autoscale because you

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:35.260
know the latencies and the
throughputs of the requests

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.060
that the application
is receiving.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.000
So that's going to provide
even more opportunities

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.460
for automation
than exists today.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.380
And Knative is really
just the start of that.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.310
The one big topic in
Kubernetes right now

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:51.994
is where to take service
discovery and load balancing

00:10:51.994 --> 00:10:52.660
into the future.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:54.534
Kubernetes has some primitives.

00:10:54.534 --> 00:10:56.200
Some of them, like
service, have existed

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.600
from the very beginning.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.400
But it has some limitations
and has grown organically.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.120
So building Istio
outside of Kubernetes

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:09.390
is what allowed us to really
innovate and experiment

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:17.100
with the service mesh approach
for providing L4 to L7 service

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.810
discovery and load balancing
between applications.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:24.810
It gives you capabilities like
monitoring the request traffic,

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.050
controlling the request
traffic, rate-limiting, even

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.810
authorizing request
traffic through identity

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:31.740
of the workloads.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:38.880
So I think one big
challenge for Kubernetes

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.680
right now is to figure out,
given the rapid development

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.380
and growing popularity
of service mesh,

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.410
how do we want to best enable
service mesh in Kubernetes

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.420
in the future?

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.750
And service mesh is designed to
work not just with Kubernetes,

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:59.010
but also with the VMs
and across environments

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.110
and across deployment
mechanisms.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:04.240
I think that's one of the
real powers about abstraction.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:07.990
And we want Kubernetes to be
a first-class part of that.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.430
And both Istio and Knative,
speaking of first-class,

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.340
have adopted the Kubernetes
resource model as a way

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:15.540
to configure those services.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.260
SAAD ALI: Right, they're
building on top of Kubernetes.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.676
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah, they're
building on-- we looked at that

00:12:19.676 --> 00:12:22.020
and said, look, these
systems need a control plane.

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.730
Well, we built a control plane.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.430
And the Kubernetes
resource model

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.190
is being used by now
hundreds of projects

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.647
to automate and control
all kinds of things.

00:12:31.647 --> 00:12:34.230
SAAD ALI: So is it fair to say
that Kubernetes is a solid base

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.186
to build on, and as
you grow your service

00:12:36.186 --> 00:12:37.560
or your application,
you're going

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.050
to begin to run into
a set of challenges

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.290
that Istio and Knative can
actually help build on top of

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.860
and solve for you?

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:47.130
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah,
I think absolutely.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:48.900
For newer users,
they might even want

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.570
to start with Knative,
which provides, for example,

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:57.060
a simple continuous
deployment solution designed

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.640
for applications like functions.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:02.430
You can plug in your
own solution there,

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:04.380
but it provides
one out of the box.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:09.382
And you can get started
using Knative now.

00:13:09.382 --> 00:13:11.340
And then if you need to
run stateful workloads,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.170
you can go right down--

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.660
talk about break the glass--
right down to Kubernetes

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.810
and run Helm Charts, or
use [INAUDIBLE] on it,

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.844
or Terraform, or
Pulumi, or whichever--

00:13:20.844 --> 00:13:22.260
or just simple
Kube-control-apply,

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:23.320
which is what I like.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:24.569
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:13:24.569 --> 00:13:26.760
If folks who aren't really
familiar with this stuff

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.000
want to find out more,
where would they go?

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.310
BRIAN GRANT: So for
the Kubernetes project,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:35.750
there's obviously
kubernetes.io, istio.io,

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.430
knative.io to learn
about those projects.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:44.400
If you want to learn about
how Google is including

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.890
these products
into its platform,

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:51.060
we have Containers homepage
on cloud.google.com.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.260
SAAD ALI: OK, that's awesome.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.920
And finally, let's finish
off talking about KubeCon.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:58.440
What are you most excited
about doing or seeing here?

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.750
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah, it's
amazing how KubeCon has grown.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.970
I was at the very
first KubeCon in 2015.

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:07.330
I had a panel on stage with
some of the other old-timers

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:07.955
on the project.

00:14:07.955 --> 00:14:10.170
I think it is less
than 500 people.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:10.770
SAAD ALI: Wow.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.500
BRIAN GRANT: And it's just
amazing to see how much it

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.930
has grown and all
the wonderful things

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.919
that people tell me that
Kubernetes is doing for them.

00:14:19.919 --> 00:14:21.960
So that's really the most
gratifying thing to me,

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:27.060
is for the people who
tell me, wow, this totally

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.820
not only made my job better,
but thereby made my life better.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:32.490
And I'm not doing
all this grudge work,

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.740
and it taking us
forever to deploy.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:38.130
We're actually able to have
much more impact on our business

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.899
by deploying much faster.

00:14:39.899 --> 00:14:41.940
And it's much less work
for me, so I can actually

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:43.703
spend the time doing
what I want to do.

00:14:43.703 --> 00:14:44.410
SAAD ALI: That is awesome.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.100
BRIAN GRANT: So it's
those stories that I love.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:46.470
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.180
Well, thank you very much
for your time, Brian.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:48.520
BRIAN GRANT: Yeah.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:49.603
SAAD ALI: Have a good one.

00:14:49.603 --> 00:14:52.470
